Title: "Boolean Set Operations"
Id: "b9b76dc4-e81e-4f52-bf7a-44aa603ed6f6"


Text: "From basic sets, we construct new sets through systematic Boolean
       operations analogous to propositional connectives. Conceived by George
       Boole in his 1854 Laws of Thought, the algebra of sets exhibits an exact
       structural isomorphism with classical propositional logic: logical
       disjunction corresponds to union, conjunction corresponds to
       intersection, and negation corresponds to complementation."
Id: "d50dcf2b-bbbf-4ded-a031-ec2f0a6d32c3"


[A \.cup./ B]
Defines: C is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "set union $A \cup B$"
. written: "A? \cup B?"
. description: "The set containing all elements belonging to set A, set B, or
                both: the least upper bound in the subset lattice."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{14}
Id: "a9493947-81ae-4cbc-be10-b8b8f6159d57"


[A \.cap./ B]
Defines: C is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "set intersection $A \cap B$"
. written: "A? \cap B?"
. description: "The set containing all elements belonging simultaneously to both
                A and B: the greatest lower bound in the subset lattice."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{15}
Id: "60d5adce-1b73-4448-8705-44bbb325a4e8"


Text: "Set difference, or relative complement, subtracts the elements of one set
       from another. Symmetric difference captures elements that belong to
       exactly one of two sets, corresponding to the Boolean exclusive-or (XOR)."
Id: "665f7f96-3ede-4ead-aa76-e67180e44fe4"


[A \.setminus./ B]
Defines: C is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "set difference $A \setminus B$"
. written: "A? \setminus B?"
. description: "The relative complement of B in A: the set of all elements in A
                that do not belong to B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{18}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{17}
Id: "f71218dd-3918-473f-89b7-1de7d8c3f511"


[A \.symm.diff./ B]
Defines: C is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "symmetric difference $A \mathbin{\Delta} B$"
. written: "A? \mathbin{\Delta} B?"
. description: "The set of elements belonging to either A or B but not both: (A
                \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A)."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{18}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{18}
Id: "aa780835-8b2c-4f88-9361-fa9873cfb871"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then: ((A \.symm.diff./ B) = ((A \.setminus./ B) \.cup./ (B \.setminus./ A)))
Documented:
. called: "symmetric difference decomposition"
. written: "A \mathbin{\Delta} B = (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A)"
. description: "Decomposing symmetric difference into the union of disjoint
                relative complements."
Proof: """An element $x$ belongs to $A \mathbin{\Delta} B$ if and only if
          $x \in A \cup B$ and $x \notin A \cap B$. This condition is logically
          equivalent to
          $(x \in A \land x \notin B) \lor (x \in B \land x \notin A)$, which is
          precisely the definition of
          $x \in (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A)$. By Extensionality, the
          two sets are identical."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{18}
Id: "e8479484-ad51-4cc9-ba51-e2f97edfd620"


Text: "When all sets under investigation are subsets of a designated universal
       set U, the complement of subset A relative to U is called the absolute
       complement of A, denoted A^c = U \setminus A."
Id: "8791f84e-7552-4360-8539-52830083ca2a"


[\absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U}]
Defines: Ac is \set
when:
. A is \set
. U is \set
Documented:
. called: "absolute complement $A^c$ in universal set $U$"
. written: "{A?}^c"
. description: "The complement of subset A with respect to ambient universal set
                U: U \setminus A."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{19}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "d382c107-43f1-4ccf-ac2b-0438a6e1f972"


Theorem:
given:
. A, U is \set
where:
. A \.subseteq./ U
then:
. (\absolute.complement:of{\absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U}}:in{U} = A)
Documented:
. called: "involution of complementation"
. written: "(A^c)^c = A"
. description: "Complementing twice relative to universe U recovers the original
                set."
Proof: """For any element $x \in U$, $x \in (A^c)^c \iff x \in U \setminus A^c
          \iff x \in U \land \neg(x \in A^c) \iff x \in U \land \neg(x \in U \land x \notin A)$.
          Since $A \subseteq U$, this simplifies to $x \in U \land x \in A \iff x \in A$.
          By Extensionality, $(A^c)^c = A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "983c6114-e31b-4a03-8e93-82ec208db82d"


Theorem:
given: U is \set
then:
. (\absolute.complement:of{\empty.set}:in{U} = U)
. (\absolute.complement:of{U}:in{U} = \empty.set)
Documented:
. called: "boundary complements"
. written: "\emptyset^c = U \land U^c = \emptyset"
. description: "The complement of the empty set is the entire universe, and the
                complement of the universe is empty."
Proof: """By definition, $\emptyset^c = U \setminus \emptyset = \{x \in U \mid x \notin \emptyset\}$.
          Since $x \notin \emptyset$ is identically true for all $x$,
          $\{x \in U \mid \text{true}\} = U$. Similarly,
          $U^c = U \setminus U = \{x \in U \mid x \notin U\}$, which has no
          members and is thus $\emptyset$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "a572985a-b09d-41d1-8e51-80b8ed62e66c"


Text: "Set operations satisfy exact algebraic symmetries directly mirroring
       propositional logic. Union and intersection are commutative and
       associative, and each operation is idempotent."
Id: "11683453-006a-4c7d-ba9c-cb48fb60bde4"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then:
. ((A \.cup./ B) = (B \.cup./ A))
. ((A \.cap./ B) = (B \.cap./ A))
Documented:
. called: "commutativity of set union and intersection"
. written: "A \cup B = B \cup A \land A \cap B = B \cap A"
. description: "Symmetry of binary lattice operations: order of operands does
                not affect the resulting union or intersection."
Proof: """By definition, $x \in A \cup B \iff x \in A \lor x \in B$. Since
          logical disjunction is commutative,
          $x \in A \lor x \in B \iff x \in B \lor x \in A \iff x \in B \cup A$.
          By Extensionality, $A \cup B = B \cup A$. Symmetrically,
          $x \in A \cap B \iff x \in A \land x \in B \iff x \in B \land x \in A \iff x \in B \cap A$,
          so $A \cap B = B \cap A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "8e9fb35c-e79a-4126-b989-03299def1991"


Theorem:
given: A, B, C is \set
then:
. (((A \.cup./ B) \.cup./ C) = (A \.cup./ (B \.cup./ C)))
. (((A \.cap./ B) \.cap./ C) = (A \.cap./ (B \.cap./ C)))
Documented:
. called: "associativity of set union and intersection"
. written: "(A \cup B) \cup C = A \cup (B \cup C) \land (A \cap B) \cap C = A
            \cap (B \cap C)"
. description: "Grouping of operations does not affect compound unions or
                intersections."
Proof: """For any $x$, $x \in (A \cup B) \cup C \iff (x \in A \lor x \in B) \lor x \in C
          \iff x \in A \lor (x \in B \lor x \in C) \iff x \in A \cup (B \cup C)$
          by associativity of logical disjunction. The intersection identity
          follows symmetrically from associativity of logical conjunction."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "37f2aea8-bf02-4bda-a310-fc0d764a54fb"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then:
. ((A \.cup./ A) = A)
. ((A \.cap./ A) = A)
. ((A \.cup./ (A \.cap./ B)) = A)
. ((A \.cap./ (A \.cup./ B)) = A)
Documented:
. called: "idempotence and absorption laws for sets"
. written: "A \cup A = A \land A \cap A = A \land A \cup (A \cap B) = A \land A
            \cap (A \cup B) = A"
. description: "Idempotence and lattice absorption identities for subsets."
Proof: """Idempotence follows from the propositional tautologies $P \lor P \iff P$
          and $P \land P \iff P$. For absorption,
          $x \in A \cup (A \cap B) \iff x \in A \lor (x \in A \land x \in B) \iff x \in A$.
          Dually, $x \in A \cap (A \cup B) \iff x \in A \land (x \in A \lor x \in B) \iff x \in A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "c73a775b-e9b5-4ef6-93f3-8a30184d1fa4"


Text: "Set operations satisfy mutual distributivity: intersection distributes
       over union, and union distributes over intersection."
Id: "989db056-b440-47a5-9b38-c6e2a9475afa"


[\distributive.law.sets]
Theorem:
given: A, B, C is \set
then:
. ((A \.cap./ (B \.cup./ C)) = ((A \.cap./ B) \.cup./ (A \.cap./ C)))
. ((A \.cup./ (B \.cap./ C)) = ((A \.cup./ B) \.cap./ (A \.cup./ C)))
Documented:
. called: "mutual distributivity of union and intersection"
. written: "A \cap (B \cup C) = (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C) \land A \cup (B \cap
            C) = (A \cup B) \cap (A \cup C)"
. description: "The algebraic distributive laws of set intersection and union."
Proof: """An element $x$ belongs to $A \cap (B \cup C)$ if and only if
          $x \in A \land (x \in B \lor x \in C)$. By the distributive law of
          propositional logic, this is equivalent to
          $(x \in A \land x \in B) \lor (x \in A \land x \in C) \iff x \in (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)$.
          The second identity follows dually from the distributivity of
          disjunction over conjunction."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{20}
Id: "54ef7b69-c0c3-4185-9b0d-f003e0293f1b"


Text: "The duality between union and intersection is captured by De Morgan's
       Laws for sets: the complement of a union is the intersection of the
       complements, and the complement of an intersection is the union of the
       complements."
Id: "e31726c2-3dc3-4c12-b205-ee6700476cc9"


[\de.morgan.set.union]
Theorem:
given: A, B, U is \set
then:
. (\absolute.complement:of{(A \.cup./ B)}:in{U} = (\absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U} \.cap./ \absolute.complement:of{B}:in{U}))
Documented:
. called: "De Morgan's First Law for Sets"
. written: "(A \cup B)^c = A^c \cap B^c"
. description: "The complement of a union is the intersection of the individual
                complements."
Proof: """For any element $x \in U$, $x \in (A \cup B)^c \iff x \in U \land \neg(x \in A \lor x \in B)$.
          By propositional De Morgan, $\neg(P \lor Q) \iff \neg P \land \neg Q$.
          Thus $x \in U \land (\neg(x \in A) \land \neg(x \in B)) \iff (x \in U \land x \notin A) \land (x \in U \land x \notin B) \iff x \in A^c \cap B^c$.
          By Extensionality, $(A \cup B)^c = A^c \cap B^c$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{20}
Id: "44f801af-ef75-423f-9d0c-b61fb58bc265"


[\de.morgan.set.intersection]
Theorem:
given: A, B, U is \set
then:
. (\absolute.complement:of{(A \.cap./ B)}:in{U} = (\absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U} \.cup./ \absolute.complement:of{B}:in{U}))
Documented:
. called: "De Morgan's Second Law for Sets"
. written: "(A \cap B)^c = A^c \cup B^c"
. description: "The complement of an intersection is the union of the individual
                complements."
Proof: """For any $x \in U$, $x \in (A \cap B)^c \iff x \in U \land \neg(x \in A \land x \in B)$.
          Applying propositional De Morgan yields
          $x \in U \land (\neg(x \in A) \lor \neg(x \in B)) \iff (x \in U \land x \notin A) \lor (x \in U \land x \notin B) \iff x \in A^c \cup B^c$.
          Extensionality completes the proof."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{20}
Id: "30e886dd-37b3-44e7-89d6-39c1a6f03abb"


Text: "Subset inclusion can be characterized through algebraic identities,
       establishing the bridge between the order-theoretic and algebraic
       perspectives on set theory."
Id: "52455c9f-4c82-4f54-956e-f2362ba2a7f9"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
where:
. A \.subseteq./ B
then:
. ((A \.cap./ B) = A)
. ((A \.cup./ B) = B)
. ((A \.setminus./ B) = \empty.set)
Documented:
. called: "equational characterizations of subset inclusion"
. written: "A \subseteq B \iff A \cap B = A \iff A \cup B = B \iff A \setminus B
            = \emptyset"
. description: "Translating containment into lattice meet, join, and difference
                equations."
Proof: """Assume $A \subseteq B$. If $x \in A \cap B$, then $x \in A$.
          Conversely, if $x \in A$, then since $A \subseteq B$, $x \in B$, so
          $x \in A \cap B$. Thus $A \cap B = A$. Similarly, $A \cup B = B$
          because every element of $A$ is already in $B$. Finally,
          $A \setminus B = \{x \in A \mid x \notin B\} = \emptyset$ because no
          element of $A$ can fail to belong to $B$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{20}
Id: "a5376470-2bbb-407d-9b53-8b6711e382a8"


Theorem:
given: A, B, C is \set
where:
. A \.subseteq./ B
then:
. ((A \.cup./ C) \.subseteq./ (B \.cup./ C))
. ((A \.cap./ C) \.subseteq./ (B \.cap./ C))
Documented:
. called: "monotonicity of union and intersection"
. written: "A \subseteq B \implies (A \cup C \subseteq B \cup C \land A \cap C
            \subseteq B \cap C)"
. description: "Order-preserving functoriality of set operations with respect to
                inclusion."
Proof: """Assume $A \subseteq B$. If $x \in A \cup C$, then $x \in A$ or
          $x \in C$. If $x \in A$, then $x \in B$ since $A \subseteq B$, so
          $x \in B \cup C$. If $x \in C$, then immediately $x \in B \cup C$.
          Thus $A \cup C \subseteq B \cup C$. For intersection, if
          $x \in A \cap C$, then $x \in A$ and $x \in C$. Since $A \subseteq B$,
          $x \in B$, so $x \in B \cap C$. Thus $A \cap C \subseteq B \cap C$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{20}
Id: "33545b66-5f0f-4ec7-ab63-71086f54aeb9"


Text: "Under these operations, the power set \mathcal{P}(U) forms a Boolean
       algebra (\mathcal{P}(U), \cup, \cap, {}^c, \emptyset, U). The empty set
       acts as zero (bottom), U acts as unit (top), and each subset A satisfies
       the complementation laws."
Id: "b59a56b3-5aa5-4276-9bd9-923e17540a44"


Theorem:
given: A, U is \set
where:
. A \.subseteq./ U
then:
. ((A \.cup./ \absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U}) = U)
. ((A \.cap./ \absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U}) = \empty.set)
Documented:
. called: "complementation identities of the Boolean algebra of sets"
. written: "A \cup A^c = U \land A \cap A^c = \emptyset"
. description: "The fundamental lattice complementation laws guaranteeing that a
                subset and its complement partition the universal set."
Proof: """Let $x \in U$. By excluded middle, either $x \in A$ or $x \notin A$.
          If $x \in A$, $x \in A \cup A^c$. If $x \notin A$, then
          $x \in U \setminus A = A^c$, so $x \in A \cup A^c$. Thus
          $A \cup A^c = U$. For intersection, an element $x \in A \cap A^c$ must
          satisfy $x \in A \land x \notin A$, which is impossible by
          non-contradiction. Thus $A \cap A^c = \emptyset$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{21}
Id: "d401689f-279d-4aec-ad59-e453aaba9806"
